{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages","id":1556472,"title":"Estimating garou population percentages","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-12-11T07:26:34-08:00","id":1556472,"title":"Estimating garou population percentages","content":"As my old correspondent <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3046_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3046\" data-vbnamecard=\"3046\">Black Fox</a> has noted at various points, the classic \u20181 Garou for every 500,000 Humans\u2019 ratio is rather flawed by the fact that it does not account for the fact that Garou tend to prefer places with fewer people and more quiet spaces (Amongst other things).<br /> <br /> Being recently reminded of this on a VAMPIRE: THE MASQUERADE thread, it occurred to me that for the sake of forming more accurate off-the-cuff estimates it would make sense to add a modifier to the 1: 500,000 ratio, revising the numbers up and down based on the population density of a given area (For example, if the human population density per square mile enters double figures subtract a percentage from estimates of the local Garou population, but a single-figure population density adds a percentage?).<br /> <br /> The problem is that I\u2019m not very good at arithmetic and would greatly appreciate advice on the most sensible way to calculate this modifier.<br /> <br /> Please note that this modifier would be intended to make quick estimates a bit more accurate, not produce in-depth calculations of the werewolf population (Also, I make no mention of wolf populations simply because human population figures are always easier to find than those of wolves).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1},{"type":"\u2615","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-11T07:52:29-08:00","id":1556473,"title":"","content":"It might be better just to assume that every almost every sept is understaffed, and judge from caern locations. Failing that, there was a section in the Revised Storyteller's Handbook that addressed this and linked some portion of their numbers to human population growth (since the majority of them are born to humans).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-11T10:46:43-08:00","id":1556479,"title":"","content":"The books have never really pushed the idea that there's a Garou:Human ratio like VtM did. WtA has always - as Saur Ops points to - gone for a more narrative metric.<br /> <br /> As well, the idea that Garou gravitate towards low population density isn't really true. The two largest Gaian Tribes (how many BSDs there are is an entirely different issue) are the ones most comfortable living in high population density areas, while the two smallest Tribes avoid said areas for entirely different reasons. The majority of the Tribes also consider specific ethnic clusters to be good for their Pure Breed through a high number of generations of drawing their Kin from those human groups. American Fianna are generally presented as more urban-friendly then their counterparts in Ireland, the UK, and mainland Europe, because most of their human Kin ended up settling in higher population density areas when arriving in the US in large numbers.<br /> <br /> Population density itself is also a tricky number to work with because how you measure it is going to skew your results heavily. If you do it by country, Ireland is twice as population dense as the US. But Dublin is between Boston and Miami in terms of population density on a finer scale. Denmark's population density varies drastically if you include Greenland or not (cutting Alaska from the US would has a less drastic but still noticeable change of a similar nature).<br /> <br /> Ultimately, to make this approximation reflective enough of WtA's setting, you'd need to decide on the average Garou preference regarding how evenly a population is distributed in a given area, not just it's density, because humans do not spread out like a gas in a confined space. It could be more helpful to consider metrics of the area's proportion of urban-to-rural populations rather than density itself because that's probably more useful. A high population density area, but that population is packed into ~10% of the Garou's &quot;territory&quot; would provide them plenty of areas to be away from humans when needed without losing access to humans when that's desired; especially compared to an area where the same population density is spread out more evenly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-11T12:42:49-08:00","id":1556482,"title":"","content":"Not much to go by actually<br /> But there are some dusty old numbers and we can build from there<br /> Rage Acros New York for example<br /> <b>New York State </b>have... had around 375 (give or take a few) Garou (17 Black Furies, around 60 Bone Gnawers, 33 Children of Gaia, 30 Fianna, 34 Get of Fenris, 52 Glass Walkers, 11 Red Talons (wow) , 23 Shadow Lords, 5 Silent Striders, 18 Silver Fangs, 2 Stargazers who came and go, regularly dropped by to visit, 36 Uktena, 36 Wendigos - and a dozen or so Black Spiral Dancers, but lets not coutn them)<br /> In 1992 - when Rage Across New York came out - New York State's population was estimated to be around <b>18.1 to 18.2 million</b>,<br /> So round it a bit, 18 million<br /> So that is, sort of kind of 1 Garou per 48,000 humans<br /> If you take New York State<br /> <br /> On the other hand Appalachia had around two houndred Garou. And while an exact 1995 -when Rage Across Apalachia came out - figure isn't readily available, Appalachia's population was around <b>23.2 million in the 1990-1999 period.</b> Lets round it to 23 million<br /> 1 Garou per 115,000 humans<br /> \u200b<br /> Whole Australia? Almost four houndred Garou altogether. Australia's population in 1994 - when Rage Across Australia came out - was approximately 17.89 million, lets use 18 million again, and 400 garou.<br /> Thats 1 Garou per 45,000 humans<br /> <br /> Using these three as a starting point, that is roughly <b>82,200 Garou worldwide</b>, which corresponds to <b>~1 Garou per 69,300 humans</b>.\u200b<br /> <br /> These are VERY rough and outdated numbers\u200b<br /> <br /> And if you add Rage Across Russia, it had around 1000 Garou in Russia. Russia's population peaked in 1993 at around <b>148 million</b> and toss it in the mix, that would change the numbers (lowering the global Garou population to around 64,000 - frankly quite generous number given their lifestyle, reproduction rate and all. And that would be ~ 1 : 89,000 - so we are creeping up to the 1:100.000 ratio the vampires had in <i>theory</i>)<br /> <br /> Again, very rough and outdated numbers and the math is not necesarily mathing just using the only basically cannon sources of areas and garou numbers<br /> <br /> But a good start to form a picture maybe, sort of kind of, possibly what numbers we could work with\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-11T18:26:58-08:00","id":1556491,"title":"","content":"Jumping off of what Heavy Arms was saying, that does seem to suggest that there\u2019s some interesting tensions going on in where Garou live and their population numbers.<br /> <br /> Garou are generally associated with wildernesses, particularly true wildernesses (ideologically loaded terms I know, but work with me), which in North America, compared with even 1,00 years ago, much less 200 or more years ago would be significantly larger. This often gets invoked to discuss the significance of wolf populations, which in North America has been largely reduced to the less developed areas of northern Canada, Alaska, and particularly rugged or intentionally preserved sections of the Western United States. <br /> <br /> The idea that such places with significantly vast wilderness areas, especially if that includes an active wolf population, would see a relatively high concentration of Garou and Caerns of significant power. <br /> <br /> But then you add the wrinkle that the overwhelming majority of contemporary Garou are said to be human born and Heavy Arm\u2019s observation about the largest Gaian tribes (the Bone Gnawers and Children of Gaia iirc) and their tolerance or even penchant for urban environments. Plus the practical arithmetic that if most Garou are human-born, it would make sense if most were born in areas closer to where the most people live, urban areas and the surrounding suburban penumbras and extensions.<br /> <br /> That would seem to suggest to me that for those rural areas, where presumably the most dedicated and active (and traditional) garou are, they either reproduce via maintaining relatively small but stable populations of kinfolk within some proximity to those wilderness septs or they semi-regularly send out bachelor/casanova wolves to areas of higher human population density in order to \u2018sow wild oats\u2019 and eventually reintegrate any successful products of that process back into the Nation.<br /> <br /> An idea I wonder about related to this is what proportion of the BG/CoG membership should we imagine as immediate descendants of those more traditional and rural septs who for whatever reason reject or are rejected by their septs and thus end up joining the more cosmopolitan tribes and how many are garou born into those tribes and their surrounding base of kinfolk. One could probably ask a similar question about the kinfolk of those tribes, how many of them are either direct or indirect descendants of kinfolk leaving behind more traditional septs and how recently.<br /> <br /> I think that could be an interesting dynamic to play with: emphasizing that these deeply rural septs are more likely to be traditional and high-prestiege (within the reward system of the Nation) and probably gather highly active and driven Garou true believers because of that but are also at risk of being somewhat brittle or fragile in a sense compared to the larger and more cosmopolitan septs in proximity to large concentrations of humanity but which might host significant numbers of \u201cEaster-and-Christmas\u201d garou who aren\u2019t interested in dedicating themselves 24/7 to the Cause (which the Bone Gnawers have certainly been accused of in different places).<br /> <br /> Anyway, this was a bit stream of consciousness, but I hope there are some thoughts in it others might find useful or interesting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-11T19:00:44-08:00","id":1556495,"title":"","content":"We have to start out understanding some bullet-points about Garou population:<ul><li> They don't depend on humans for sustenance or any other vital resource, so while humans may help or hinder their goals, there's no ecological pressure like there is with vampires;</li> <li> Garou can be born from any human or wolf, with Kinfolk being the <i>main</i> sources, but not the only ones;</li> <li> Kinfolk don't suffer any population pressure from normal humans, either, they may exist in any number among their respective populations, up to and including everyone if you want;</li> <li> At the end of the day this is a spiritual process. <i>Deus Ex Machina</i> may interfere to make them be born more or less frequently for whatever reasons;</li> <li> More important to the setting than their ratio to humans is their ratio to work. It may well be that Garou are being born in lower and lower numbers even as human population grows;</li> <li> Population fluctuates, and for Garou more than any other splat. With the highest friction among them all and numbers that replenish constantly, their numbers and members can change drastically from a year to the next.</li> </ul><br /> At the end of the day I think the whole idea of giving hard numbers to the whole state/area is foolish.<br /> <br /> Jot down the main Caerns in an area and main sources of problems, have an idea of how many Garou would be necessary at each main sect for it to deal comfortably with their issues, and give then <i>less</i>. How much less sets the tone for the setting, which is what really matter. Then just describe some of those main sects as a snapshot in time and that's that, don't bother with numbers outside them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-11T20:44:42-08:00","id":1556499,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#e74c3c\"><b><span style=\"font-family:Comic Sans MS\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Feel free to completely ignore my post, because this is just me yapping.</span></span></b></span><br /> Kinfolk usually are scaled as &quot;1 in 10,000 in either human or wolf population numbers&quot;, at most.<br /> <br /> However... <b>I'm a little chaos gremlin</b>, so for a Chronicle that I and a friend are working on, we made it 1 in 80, as a VERY SPECIFIC phenomenon to a VERY SPECIFIC geographical region.<br /> <br /> The idea being that Kinfolk are being \u201cswitched on,\u201d not \u201cbred in\u201d at this geographical location.<br /> <br /> Resulting Quote:<br /> \u201cSTOP FINDING CHILDREN IN THE WOODS, YOU\u2019RE SUPPOSED TO BE AT WAR!\u201d<br /> <br /> <br /> Also: This entire explanation might be a load of hokey-nothing, and there MIGHT have been a Mage this whole time, employed by Pentex with high levels in the Entropy, Correspondence, and Time spheres to make sure that this happens 100% of the time, to prevent the Garou from getting anything done whatsoever (as well as doing &quot;just a little bit of trolling&quot; - because I love injecting bits of absurdist humor into my games every now-and-then, and Mages are perfect for that in a meta-sense because of how easy it is for any situation involving them to go to clown town).<br /> This is also the same draft where Vampires are hunting Fomori for masquerade violations and Imbued Hunters are the new modern Urban Cryptids. lol <hr /><br /> <b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Anyway, where were we before I came in like Harrier Dubois after a cosmic bender?</span></b><br /> <br /> <br /> Ah yes - <u><b>Garou and Kinfolk Population percentages:</b></u><br /> Trevor Belmont's family situation from S1 of the netflix anime is a (patchy, yet functional) example of a kinfolk family situation - even though his family would be more &quot;in-place&quot; in a Dark Ages: Inquisitor game with the Religious Heritage flaw (p167 for those w/ the book).<br /> <br /> <br /> The game never gives strict global numbers (which I think is a good thing), but the accepted Storyteller estimate is something like this (please take w/ grain of salt, because I'm probably wrong):<br /> <br /> Garou worldwide = ~10,000\u201312,000<br /> <br /> Garou kinfolk (human + wolf, total) = ~100,000\u2013150,000<br /> <br /> Each Fera species (assuming they're around in your chronicle) = a few hundred globally<br /> <br /> Fera kinfolk = often fewer than 1,000 per breed<br /> <br /> If you think its too many or too much, then feel free to grab a calculator and adjust as you see fit.<br /> <br /> As I said, I'm a massive chaos gremlin who wants to explore ideas where the cannon setting splats' expectations of the metaplot and &quot;ultimate end&quot; get challenged in ways where the only appropriate response is to be completely flabbergasted.<br /> <br /> I might edit this comment later, but this is all I have to say as of now.<br /> <br /> <br /> EDIT: Better information is now available in the thread!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28838,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","user":"DaFrontlineSpecter","date":".  Too much nonsense even for me. 12-12-2025, 06:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","name":"DaFrontlineSpecter","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-12T02:09:26-08:00","id":1556506,"title":"","content":"Those numbers are all extremely low, and besides the Garou ones, I have no idea where you might be getting them when it comes to the actual books. Esp. when it comes to Garou kinfolk, your numbers basically mean wolf-kin don't exist unless you expand the species that count as viable kin... and then it's &quot;essentially non-existent&quot; to, &quot;most Garou will only hear about them barely existent.&quot; For the Red Talons to exist they'd pretty much have to have a lock of all of the wolf-kin and the small boost they have in Africa thanks to the Mokole. While 1:80 is probably too common for the books' intentions, if you account for the number of unknowing Kin likely out there... it really isn't that far out of a number. Just to keep the number of Garou stable, you'd need 10x to 20x the Garou population in the number of kin actively trying to procreate more Garou. When you factor in non-procreating kin - like children too young to do so - the total number of kin would need to be much higher than just 10x the Garou population.<br /> <br /> The rest of the shifters are hard to quantify in the same terms as the Garou because they have their own narrative roles that has lead to wildly different takes on their expected populations, their expected kin populations, and how many kin there are per shifter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-12T10:24:29-08:00","id":1556521,"title":"","content":"I'll give this a more thorough follow up, mostly because I'm in the mood.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages?p=1556499#post1556499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Kinfolk usually are scaled as &quot;1 in 10,000 in either human or wolf population numbers&quot;, at most.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't remember this being stated anywhere, although it may not be a bad assumption at least for <u><b>known</b></u> <b>HUMAN </b>kin. I think this is too conservative, though.<br /> <br /> For wolves, that would leave us with around 20 to 25 wolf kin in the whole world, orders of magnitude less than the number of Wolf-Born Garou.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages?p=1556499#post1556499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">for a Chronicle that I and a friend are working on, we made it 1 in 80, as a VERY SPECIFIC phenomenon to a VERY SPECIFIC geographical region.<br /> <br /> The idea being that Kinfolk are being \u201cswitched on,\u201d not \u201cbred in\u201d at this geographical location.</div> </div> </div> </div>Perfectly fair, it could be even higher, though.<br /> <br /> I don't think 1:80 really gives you the effect you want, especially if this is a recent phenomenon.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages?p=1556499#post1556499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">SPIRIT LEAK + THIN GAUNTLET = ETHNIC AWAKENING OF LATENT LINES</div> </div> </div> </div>Not by itself a very logical conclusion, to begin with. Exposure to the Umbra or spirits does not increase or cause such a change.<br /> <br /> While the phenomena may still be there, it's too obviously not the cause.<br /> <br /> Second, though, the ethnic part may be a problem. Depending on how you do it, a very big problem.<br /> <br /> The game does link Tribes and Kinfolk to ethnicity, but it's purposefully not in a big way and spreading them through all kinds of folks, so this is more of a &quot;family matter&quot; than an &quot;ethnic phenomenon&quot;.<br /> <br /> If the difference becomes massive enough for Kinfolk to be clearly linked to some ethnic groups and not others, that raises far more questions and consequences than you're thinking.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages?p=1556499#post1556499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">- Centuries-old Kinfolk lines that \u201cforgot what they were\u201d are waking back up</div> </div> </div> </div>There's no direct relationship between memory/culture and being kinfolk. Plenty of families are kin without knowing it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages?p=1556499#post1556499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">- Dormant wolf-blood genetics get recognized by spirits</div> </div> </div> </div>There's no wolf-blood genetics, Garou and Kinfolk have no genetic marker, the game is frequently adamant on saying that DNA (the in-game company) is wrong and it has nothing to do with biology, much less genetics.<br /> <br /> It's a purely spiritual condition already. You don't need any particular biological make-up to be born Kinfolk, despite how much whatever spiritual force behind it likes to keep it in families.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages?p=1556499#post1556499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">- Old pacts, oaths, or tribal affinities reassert themselves</div> </div> </div> </div>Those rarely if ever affect kinfolk in any way.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages?p=1556499#post1556499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">- Ancestral spirits \u201cclaim\u201d descendants through dreams, omens, or odd events</div> </div> </div> </div>Already a thing, while the increase in frequency may be relevant, by itself it's not new.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages?p=1556499#post1556499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">- Human families discover Garou ancestry that was dormant for like, 2\u201320 generations\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>From some point between 10 and 20 generations ago, everyone alive today descends from a Garou.<br /> <br /> About 40 generations is the Genetic Isopoint for European Ancestry. That means <i><b>everyone</b></i> with a modicum of European ancestry today (which is virtually everyone now, even isolates) is descended from <i><b>absolutely everyone</b></i> from European stock alive around 1.000 AD. Literally everyone here descends from Charlemagne, no exceptions. Your proposal (being descended from <i><b>any</b></i> Garou instead of <i><b>all</b></i> Garou) is orders of magnitude easier.<br /> <br /> That doesn't means we're all genetically related, because genes don't work like that and you lack <b><i>any</i></b> genetic marker whatsoever from half your ancestors from as early as 11 generations before. But then, Kin is not a matter of genetics and this <i><b>is</b></i> enough for you to be born kin. It happens.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages?p=1556499#post1556499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u201cSTOP FINDING CHILDREN IN THE WOODS, YOU\u2019RE SUPPOSED TO BE AT WAR!\u201d\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>By itself alone, it's unlikely that the Garou will spend so much time searching for Lost Kin instead of doing everything else they have to do.<br /> <br /> Lost kin is already a thing, and a big thing. It's plausible that they may even outnumber known kin, especially on big cities. We're talking here that are still born Kin and capable of siring Garou at any moment, but are completely oblivious and unknown by the Garou. Werewolves already try to find them, already can't deal with the sheer frequency it happens, and already stopped looking for them instead of doing their job.<br /> <br /> You'll get more mileage and more interesting and setting-relevant results if you explore other problems arising with that:<br /> <br /> Kinfolk don't suffer <i>Delirium</i>, and people who won't go bonkers upon watching a Crinos are a problem. They're also unlikely to talk as they still see the Garou as motherfucking monsters that shouldn't be possible and are merciless killing machines, they just don't <b><u>completely</u></b> lose it and remember. No few Werewolf hunters are Kinfolk, most of those being Lost Kin.<br /> <br /> How much of a problem do the local Septs have when a sizable portion of the population suddenly becomes free of <i>Delirium</i>?<br /> <br /> If this has been happening for a time, now, what about Lost Cubs and Lunatics? Finding every Kinfolk around isn't something the Garou will really waste their time doing, Kinfolk can do it. Finding new Garou is another matter entirely. Uneducated cubs can cause a huge load of problems, especially if they start to show up faster than the Garou can keep with it. Those who achieve to suppress the First Change altogether in the process are rare, but potentially worse, and also a loss to the Nation.<br /> <br /> And how much will the BSD be better at capitalizing on the situation? Not only they don't care about many of those problems, so won't waste resources trying to deal with them, they actually have an invested interest in bringing any and every asset without much in terms of proper handling, and uses even for the Lunatics, if they're not actually in a better position to find all those new Kin and Garou to begin with.<br /> <br /> There's a lot you can explore here beyond just Garou too occupied with wandering Kinfolk (that may not even be retrievable to them whatsoever, most people won't join your secret family cult of savage beasts just because they're related through some forgotten ancestor).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages?p=1556499#post1556499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">there MIGHT have been a Mage this whole time, employed by Pentex with high levels in the Entropy, Correspondence, and Time spheres to make sure that this happens 100% of the time</div> </div> </div> </div>Hardly doable without Spirit.<br /> <br /> This is not a pure chance phenomenon, the Spirit World simply don't work like that, even when it does (yes, it kinda does, but it's complicated).<br /> <br /> As said before, this is not just a genetics matter to begin with. It's also not a random lightning or something. The Spirit works through meaning and purpose, and the <b>Spirit Sphere</b> will deal with that in this particular sense.<br /> <br /> Without it, <b>Time</b> and <b>Entropy</b> will likely just give you that there's no timeline where the target becomes Kin, because there's no reason for them to, whatever the hidden reasoning here is. They can't make an event happen if it isn't a potential in the first place. <b>Correspondence</b>... can't do much to begin with, I guess? Maybe reinforce sympathetic connections can help, but I think that's a bit of a stretch. The three together would be able to find suitable targets for the phenomenon and suitable surges of the phenomenon, and then make the target &quot;be there&quot; in some sense, but that would still be less effective than doing the same thing with <b>Spirit</b> and wouldn't really do much at all.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages?p=1556499#post1556499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Garou worldwide = ~10,000\u201312,000</div> </div> </div> </div>The most conservative estimations are 2 Garou for every million humans, so about 16.000 today, but those are extremely conservative and completely ignored by the books themselves. They usually work with something between 80.000 and 160.000 Garou worldwide if we assume local populations as at least close to an average.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages?p=1556499#post1556499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Garou kinfolk (human + wolf, total) = ~100,000\u2013150,000</div> </div> </div> </div>What? Where did you get this from?<br /> <br /> Just under the assumption of a 1:10.000 ratio we would have 800.000 kinfolk at the very least, add Lost Kin and that's easy above 1 million.<br /> <br /> I think at least 20\u00d7Garou Population is a good starting point, so ~1.600.000 and 3.200.000 known kin all over the world, and that's just human.<br /> <br /> Calculating Wolf kin is trickier, but we can look at some numbers and see that, for example, if there's less than 1:100 then most US states with sizable wolf populations would still get none at all. This is not a stable population for any metric.<br /> <br /> Between 1:10 and 1:100 gives you a wide berth to work it that still keeps to the notions of them being in danger, but still there enough for them to be worth protecting and Wolf-Born being, well, born.<br /> <br /> The current world wolf population lies between 200.000 and 250.000 as of December 2025 in the real world. The above ratios would mean something between 2.000 and 25.000 all around the world. Keep in mind that the game usually assumes the Wolf-Born to be something around 10% of Garou population, so we're talking 1.600 under the 2:1m ratio, or 8.000 to 16.000 for the observed ratios, and a kin population below 10\u00d7Garou is dangerous, which is an assumption of the setting anyway, but we're dealing with exceedingly low numbers here.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages?p=1556499#post1556499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Each Fera species (assuming they're around in your chronicle) = a few hundred globally</div> </div> </div> </div>Exceedingly variable. It isn't even guaranteed that Garou are really the most numerous.<br /> <br /> Ratkins and Ananasi are both very likely to exceed Werewolves, maybe by orders of magnitude. Rokea are immortal, so their number isn't really tied to the number of sharks. Most others are deemed rare, but &quot;rare&quot; isn't a number you can be sure of. Only the Nuwisha are given an exact number of 100 <i>on Earth</i>.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1556472-estimating-garou-population-percentages?p=1556499#post1556499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Fera kinfolk = often fewer than 1,000 per breed</div> </div> </div> </div>Really unlikely, especially when we consider that they're not guaranteed to &quot;breed true&quot; at the same proportion Garou currently do. Depending on many things, they may be more than 100 for every shifter of the species.<br /> <br /> Those shifters suffered under Garou assault, but their kin didn't. In some cases their kin are far more numerous than wolves, their human kin are still a part of the exploding human population, so I don't think they're few, they just don't breed true frequently, and probably most are Lost Kin or at least ignorant kin, watched but not informed.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-12T12:42:19-08:00","id":1556536,"title":"","content":"I wanted to pop in and thank you all for contributing to this thread (Also to confess that I wan never looking for hard and fast numbers, only for a quick-and-dirty way to make estimates of local Garou populations without doing any detailed worldbuilding).<br /> <br /> I\u2019ll be very happy to follow the discussion and will do my best to contribute as and when one can find something useful to say.<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}